A||||9||Songman||Matching Numbers: How important is it?||||1||
Z||000000||Songman||08-17-2001||10:07 AM||||I'm going over questions in my mind. The questions are about my Indy Pace Car. I would like to redo the motor for it and freshen it up a bit, but I am wondering what the effect would be on value if I were to build my extra motor and then just drop it into the Indy. The extra motor is also out of an 84. I don't think there is a VIN stamped on the block anywhere. I could build the other motor and already have it mounted to another cradle and not have very much downtime for my car.<P>Any thoughts on this? Anyone thing having the exact original motor is a plus?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||08-17-2001||10:24 AM||||Weren't a bunch of motors replaced in the 84 cars as part of the recall, anyway? Seems like there's at least an outside chance that it already doesn't have the original, if all the recalls have been done. <BR>Just a thought...<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||08-17-2001||10:34 AM||||Good thought, Steve... I didn't know if they replaced the entire motors or just parts of them. I know that any 'recall' Indys I have ever seen had lost the original silver valve cover anyway.<P>It sure would make my life easier just to have the other motor sitting there ready to go.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||hyhy4u||08-17-2001||11:40 AM||||I dont think it will affect the value to much..I think its just more "bragging" rights to be able to say all your numbers match.  As for the stamping on the block im almost sure its gotta be there...<P> -Jason||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||08-17-2001||11:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hyhy4u:<BR><B>I dont think it will affect the value to much..I think its just more "bragging" rights to be able to say all your numbers match.  As for the stamping on the block im almost sure its gotta be there...<P> -Jason</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There is an engine identification number, but I don't think the VIN is there. They stopped doing that in the early 70s I think.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||08-17-2001||12:24 PM||||Just my opinion on "numbers matching". Right now, it doesn't really mean much. About the only time it may affect the value is on a very low mileage car. You know, one that is worthy of collecting. The Indy version is one of those cars. Unfortunately, if it's a high mileage car, numbers matching really doesn't affect the value. As a matter of fact, a high mileage Indy is probably worth more the general public if it's been converted to a V6 or V8.<P>With all that said and done, value is in the eye of the buyer. Because it's only after the sell that the true value is known.<P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||08-17-2001||12:54 PM||||i pretty much think that is reserved for high dollar cars. The only time I hear of judges checking that carefully is like for Bloomington Gold Corvettes. The really serious collectors care but thats all. On the vettes, I have a close friend whos a NCR judge, they even get new blocks manufactured with their old serial numbers in the casting to keep them 'original' ||||reg||1
Z||000007||ray b||08-17-2001||01:22 PM||||# that match are a big deal on rare collecter cars like 69 Z28 price is 1/2<BR>for remotored car even if exact same motor<BR>but unless it is a low miles or perfect resto<BR>no fiero is a real high $$$ car anyway.<BR>what is a indy worth if perfect low mile car<BR>best i saw at auction sites was 2500 for a indy at kruse site a year ago.<BR>20 years from now may be a big deal when the fiero are rare cars but not now.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000008||FIERO JOHN-WI||08-17-2001||01:32 PM||||Songman, it will matter (now) not really! (future)Oh yes and how. I don't know if you have been able to attend Pontiac Nationals, but one of the requirements if you are going into judged class and not popular vote class you must have a copy of the shipping invoice with you and something to verify mileage (emission statement) when you go to the show. As for the motor stamp (I heard) it is somewhere by the starter side of the block   (again this is what I heard) I haven't looked at mine yet, I figure next oil change I'll take look around.<BR>High mileage cars will be worth money if everything matches, especially if you enter your Fiero at the Pontiac Nationals and win the "Gold Certificate" for cars with 390 out of 400 points. Collectors and especially hobbiests are looking to buy these cars that win and do like to drive them from time to time. This is only my opinion from what I have seen, what ever you do, good luck and besides it's your car and if your never planning on selling it, do what you want.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Songman||08-17-2001||01:32 PM||||Thanks guys... About what I was figuring too! I never would have considered swapping the motor in my Judge, but I think I will on this one... Thinking of selling it anyway so making it the most drivable will probably be good...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||7
